1. Project Concept: ExpensiveManager
ExpensiveManager:
An Expense Tracking and Management System for teams & individuals, with secure APIs, multi-user support, analytics, and deployment-ready code.

2. Features & Tech Stack
Core Features
User Registration/Login (Spring Security, JWT)

Role-based access (Admin/User)

RESTful API for CRUD on Expenses, Categories, Users

Group Expenses, Analytics (stream ops, aggregation)

Database Transactions (expense transfers, group splits)

Data Validation (Spring Validation)

Exception Handling (Global advice)

Multi-threaded Report Generation (Java concurrency)

Memory Management (caching, object pooling demo)

Cloud-ready (Docker + AWS/GCP/Azure deployment)

Documentation (Swagger/OpenAPI)

Logging & Monitoring

Tech Stack
Java 8+

Spring Boot, Spring Data JPA, Spring Security

H2/PostgreSQL (dev/prod)

JWT for authentication

Maven/Gradle

Docker

Cloud: AWS Elastic Beanstalk/EC2 or GCP App Engine

Optional: Redis for caching (if you want to try)

Git (with branch strategy)

VSCode or IntelliJ

3. Software Process
Requirement Analysis & Breakdown

Architecture & Design (UML/class diagrams, REST endpoints spec)

Incremental Development (feature branches)

Code Reviews (if you want to simulate this, I can “review” your code)

Testing (unit, integration, API)

Documentation (README, API docs)

CI/CD (GitHub Actions optional)

Deployment (Docker + Cloud)

Monitoring (Actuator + Logs)

4. Phase-wise Roadmap
Phase 1: Project Setup & Design
 Define requirements & sketch entity model

 Setup Spring Boot project (Maven, modules)

 Setup GitHub repo and branching model

 Define initial DB schema (ER diagram)

 Set up README.md with project goals

Phase 2: Core Functionality
 Implement User registration/login with Spring Security + JWT

 Role-based access for APIs

 CRUD for Expense, Category, User

 RESTful API design (resource naming, HATEOAS basics)

 Data Validation on input (DTOs, BindingResult, annotations)

Phase 3: Advanced Features
 Group Expense logic (transaction management)

 Report Generation (multi-threaded, streaming)

 Caching & memory management (demo with @Cacheable or simple pooling)

 Exception Handling (global @ControllerAdvice)

 API documentation (Swagger/OpenAPI)

Phase 4: Testing & Best Practices
 Unit & integration tests (JUnit, Mockito)

 Code reviews and refactoring

 Logging & Monitoring (Spring Actuator)

Phase 5: Cloud & Deployment
 Dockerize application

 Deploy to AWS/GCP

 Add deployment documentation

5. Step 1: Define Domain & Entity Model
Let’s quickly sketch the key entities (high level):

User (id, username, email, password, role)

Expense (id, amount, description, date, userId, categoryId, groupId)

Category (id, name)

Group (id, name, memberIds)

ExpenseSplit (id, expenseId, paidBy, paidFor, amount)

AuditLog (id, entity, operation, timestamp, userId) — for bonus

